## 1. Questions

| # | Question | Answered by |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | Mean outer-fold accuracy of multinomial LR and of kNN (k chosen by inner CV), each with a 95% interval, under 10 x stratified 5-fold CV on Iris (OpenML 61) | A1-A3 |
| Q2 | Paired difference LR - kNN on the same 50 outer folds, with a 95% interval; does the interval cover 0? | A3 |
| Q3 | Does the full CV run as an Ibex CPU job give numbers identical to the full local run? On which node did it run? | A4 |
| Q4 (supporting) | Which k does the inner CV select across the 50 outer folds? | A2 |

No conclusion is presumed. The feasibility judgement expects both accuracies near 96-97% and a
difference interval that covers 0; the paper reports what is measured, whichever way it falls, and
claims nothing beyond Iris.

## 2. Design (pre-specified)

### D1 Data
- OpenML dataset id 61 (`iris`), downloaded from OpenML itself (API `www.openml.org/api/v1/json/data/61`
  for metadata; the data file from openml.org / data.openml.org). Target = OpenML default target
  attribute (`class`); 4 numeric features in file order, float64.
- Record: download URL, dataset name and version, OpenML-listed md5 vs computed md5, sha256 of the
  saved file, shape (150 x 4), class counts (expected 50/50/50). Labels encoded by sorted class name.
- Reference values from the brief: ARFF `https://openml.org/data/v1/download/61/iris.arff`, OpenML md5
  `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9`, version 1 (Parquet `https://data.openml.org/datasets/0000/0061/dataset_61.pq`
  is equally from OpenML). Not `sklearn.datasets.load_iris` (bundled copy).
- The file is used as is: its 3 exact duplicate rows are kept (they can fall in train and test of a fold)
  and its two entries that differ from Fisher's published data (UCI rows 35, 38; Bezdek et al. 1999) are not
  corrected; the paper states both in one sentence or footnote.
- Downloaded **once**; the same file is staged to Ibex. Every run checks the sha256 at start and
  aborts on mismatch.

### D2 Outer resampling (shared by both methods: paired design)
- `RepeatedStratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, n_repeats=10, random_state=20261017)` -> J = 50 outer folds,
  120 train / 30 test each (10 test cases per class).
- Both methods are fit and scored on exactly the same split indices; the test indices of every fold
  are saved.

### D3 Methods (all fitting inside each outer training fold only)
- **LR**: `Pipeline[StandardScaler, LogisticRegression(penalty="l2", C=1.0, solver="lbfgs", max_iter=10000)]`.
  Multinomial (softmax) loss: state how the installed scikit-learn version guarantees it (lbfgs is
  multinomial for >2 classes in recent versions; pass `multi_class="multinomial"` where the version
  still takes it) and check `coef_.shape == (3, 4)`. Not tuned. A `ConvergenceWarning` is an error.
- **kNN**: `Pipeline[StandardScaler, KNeighborsClassifier(weights="uniform", p=2, algorithm="brute")]`,
  k selected by `GridSearchCV` over k in {1, 3, 5, ..., 31} (16 values; 31 < 96, the smallest inner
  training size), inner `StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=1000 + f)` for outer
  fold f = 0..49, scoring accuracy, refit on the whole outer training fold.
  Tie-break: among k with equal mean inner accuracy, the **smallest** k (assert that the chosen k is
  the smallest maximiser; do not rely on an undocumented default).
- Scaling: standardisation fit on the training data only, the same for both methods (L2-penalised LR
  and Euclidean kNN are both scale dependent; Iris shares units, so this is a convention, stated).
- Determinism: `n_jobs=1`; `OMP_NUM_THREADS=OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=MKL_NUM_THREADS=1`.

### D4 Measurements per outer fold
Accuracy `a_LR,f`, `a_kNN,f` (multiples of 1/30), paired difference `d_f = a_LR,f - a_kNN,f`,
selected k, and the predicted label of every test case for both methods (plus LR predicted
probabilities, as a diagnostic for the identity check only).

### D5 Intervals
- **Primary** (all three quantities: mean a_LR, mean a_kNN, mean d): corrected resampled t
  (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; corrected repeated k-fold CV, Bouckaert & Frank 2004):
  mean x over J = 50 folds, s^2 the sample variance (ddof = 1),
  var = (1/J + n_test/n_train) * s^2 with n_test/n_train = 30/120 = 0.25,
  95% interval x +/- t(0.975, J-1) * sqrt(var).
- **Labelled comparisons** (results file always; paper only if space allows, never as the main
  interval): naive t over the 50 folds (s/sqrt(50), 49 df); t over the 10 repetition means (9 df).
- Also: counts of folds with d > 0, d = 0, d < 0.
- If s^2 = 0 the interval is degenerate and reported as such. An accuracy bound above 1 is kept
  unclipped in the results file; if the paper shows it clipped, it says so.
- Wording: the paper says whether the difference interval covers 0; it does not convert this into a
  claim of equivalence or superiority.

### D6 Ibex vs local (the identity question)
- Same code revision, same data file (sha256 checked), same locked environment on both machines:
  same Python minor version and exact numpy / scipy / scikit-learn / joblib / threadpoolctl versions
  (lock file delivered in `report/code/`; Ibex env built with micromamba or venv on the login node,
  the system Python 3.9 is not used unless the local side matches it), single-threaded as in D3.
- **Ibex**: one `sbatch` CPU job (partition `batch`, 1-4 CPUs, <= 15 min walltime), job name
  `$HT_RESOURCE_TAG-cv`, tracked with `ht-resource track`. The job output itself records `hostname`,
  `$SLURMD_NODENAME`, `$SLURM_JOB_ID`, CPU model, Python/package versions, BLAS info
  (threadpoolctl), data sha256, code revision. The reported node name is the one written by the job,
  never the login node.
- **Local**: the same script, full size, as an `ht-resource run` job (environment and data prepared
  beforehand: job containers have no network), recording the same metadata.
- Submit the Ibex job as early as possible; run the local job concurrently (the queue is the only
  schedule risk).
- **"Identical" is defined now**, on two levels, both reported:
  - L1 raw: per-fold predicted labels (50 folds x 2 methods), selected k per fold and per-fold
    accuracies exactly equal (array equality, plus sha256 of a canonical serialisation of each run's
    raw results).
  - L2 reported: every number printed in the paper equal at its printed precision.
  - Diagnostics, always reported: max |difference| of raw per-fold accuracies, of the summary numbers
    (full float precision) and of LR predicted probabilities.
  - The paper says "identical" only if L1 and L2 hold; otherwise "not identical", with what differs
    and by how much. No rerun or tuning to force agreement; a rerun after an infrastructure failure is
    allowed and logged.
- **Primary numbers** in the table and text are the **local** run's; the Ibex run is the replicate.
  If they differ, the table shows both.

